<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · region</p><h1 class="enc-title">Saga Prefecture</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Saga Prefecture is a prefecture of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/japan">Japan</a> in north-western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kyushu">Kyushu</a>, with its capital at Saga. It covers the eastern part of the former Hizen province, including the lands of the Saga domain ruled by the Nabeshima until 1871. It is known for the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/porcelain">porcelain</a> towns of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arita">Arita</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/imari">Imari</a> and for the Yoshinogari archaeological site of the Yayoi period.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/saga-prefecture.jpg" alt="Saga Prefecture" width="320" height="213" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: ascesis · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Nijinomatsubara.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Saga Prefecture occupies the north-western corner of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kyushu">Kyushu</a>, an isthmus-like area between the Genkai Sea and the Tsushima Strait to the north and the Ariake Sea to the south. It borders <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fukuoka-prefecture">Fukuoka Prefecture</a> to the northeast and Nagasaki Prefecture to the southwest and covers about 2,439 square kilometres. Its nearness to mainland <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/asia">Asia</a> made it an important gateway for the transmission of culture and trade throughout Japanese history. Outside the cities of Saga and Karatsu it is largely rural, and agricultural and forested land makes up over 68 per cent of its area.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>In ancient times the area of Saga and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nagasaki">Nagasaki</a> prefectures was called <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hizen-province">Hizen Province</a>, and the present name dates from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/meiji-restoration">Meiji Restoration</a>. Rice farming has flourished here since ancient times, and its traces survive at the ruins of Nabatake in Karatsu and at the Yoshinogari site, where villages flourished in the middle of the Yayoi period. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mongol-invasions-of-japan">Mongol invasions of Japan</a> began here with the Battle of Bun'ei in 1274 and the Battle of Kōan in 1281, and in 1591 Nagoya Castle was built for the Japanese invasions of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/korea">Korea</a>.</p><p>In the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sengoku-period">Sengoku period</a> the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ryuzoji-clan">Ryūzōji clan</a> extended its control over all of Hizen and Chikugo and parts of Higo and Chikuzen. After the death of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ryuzoji-takanobu">Ryūzōji Takanobu</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nabeshima-naoshige">Nabeshima Naoshige</a> took control, and by 1607 the whole Ryūzōji domain was in Nabeshima hands. In the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/edo-period">Edo period</a> the Saga Domain, with its three sub-domains, remained under the Nabeshima and was politically stable, though the cost of defending <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nagasaki">Nagasaki</a> and disasters such as the Kyōhō famine and the Siebold Typhoon of 1828 strained its finances. Land reclaimed from the Ariake Sea roughly doubled its yield in koku over two centuries.</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nabeshima-naomasa">Nabeshima Naomasa</a> reformed the domain's finances in the middle of the 19th century and encouraged <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arita">Arita</a> porcelain, green <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tea">tea</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/coal">coal</a>. Technologies such as the reverberatory furnace and models of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/steam-locomotive">steam locomotives</a> arrived through the nearby port of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nagasaki">Nagasaki</a>. Many people from the domain assisted the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/meiji-restoration">Meiji Restoration</a>. After the han system was abolished in 1871 the area was merged into Imari Prefecture, renamed Saga Prefecture in 1872, absorbed into Nagasaki Prefecture in 1876 and set up again as a separate prefecture in 1883. The Saga Rebellion took place in February 1874.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>The prefecture has a population of roughly 780,000. Saga is its capital and largest city, and other major cities include Karatsu, Tosu and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/imari">Imari</a>. Its western region is known for ceramics and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/porcelain">porcelain</a>, particularly in Karatsu, Imari and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arita">Arita</a>. All of its <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/coal">coal</a> mines closed in 1972 with the closing of the Nishiki mine, and the Yoshinogari History Park opened to the public in 1992.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Saga Prefecture” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Two sites have been proposed, Yoshinogari in <b>Saga Prefecture</b> and Makimuku in Nara Prefecture, and recent archaeological research at Makimuku suggests that Yamataikoku was there.</blockquote><a href="/culture/yayoi-culture">Yayoi culture · Legacy</a></li><li><blockquote>It became part of <b>Saga Prefecture</b> in 1871, and in 1874 Eto Shinpei led the failed Saga Rebellion.</blockquote><a href="/state/saga-domain">Saga Domain</a></li></ul></section>
